    "body": "Members of the family behind the collapsed R&R Family of Companies are listed as executives of a newly established Pittsburgh-area logistics business, even as former R&R chief executive Richard Stephen Francis navigates personal Chapter 11 bankruptcy proceedings in Florida.\nPennsylvania Department of State records show Giant Logistics LLC was organized on Nov. 13, 2025, with an effective date of Jan. 1, 2026.\nR&R’s operations began shutting down in January, leaving hundreds of employees without jobs and carriers and other freight-related businesses pursuing unpaid invoices .\nThe company’s organizer was listed as Richard J. Francis, the youngest son of R&R founder Rich Francis.\nA Sept. 29 annual report identifies Jonathan Francis as Giant Logistics’ president, with Richard Francis and Clint Prosperi listed as vice presidents. All three are also identified as company governors.\nJonathan R. Francis, Rich Francis’ oldest son, previously served as vice president of R&R Express’ Company Assets Division, according to his professional profile. Prosperi, another former R&R executive, is also a member of the Francis family by marriage.\nThe corporate filings establish an overlap between individuals associated with R&R and Giant Logistics, but do not establish whether the new business acquired R&R assets, customers or freight contracts.\nIn a Nov. 13 email to FreightWaves, Rich Francis said Richard J. Francis was his son and there was no connection between the two companies.\n“I have no ownership in Giant Logistics. There is no connection between R & R and Giant,” Francis wrote.\nThe elder Francis’ personal bankruptcy disclosures, however, identify Giant Logistics as a source of employment income. His filings reported approximately $37,078 in monthly income from commissioned sales work at the company.\nThe disclosures do not establish that Francis holds an ownership interest in Giant Logistics.\nBankruptcy creditor seeks financial records\nFrancis filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection Sept. 22 in the U.S. Bankruptcy Court for the Middle District of Florida.\nHis bankruptcy schedules reported approximately $12.67 million in assets and $7.46 million in liabilities, including an approximately $8.5 million residence in Marco Island, Florida.\nThe proceedings have drawn attention from creditors, including Varilease Finance Inc., which is listed in the bankruptcy’s largest unsecured creditors filing with a $10.68 million claim arising from a personal guarantee.\nOn Nov. 3, Varilease filed a notice seeking testimony and financial records from Francis as part of the bankruptcy proceedings.\nThe examination is scheduled for Dec. 10, with requested documents due Dec. 3. The creditor’s requests include information about Francis’ employment and compensation arrangements, financial transactions, property holdings and other assets.\nThe requests are part of the bankruptcy discovery process and do not constitute findings of wrongdoing.\nThe court also approved the employment of Seese, P.A., as counsel for Francis in his capacity as debtor-in-possession on Nov. 10.\nFrancis’ initial meeting of creditors was held and concluded Oct. 27. The U.S. trustee has indicated that no official committee of creditors will be appointed until further notice.\nThe bankruptcy docket lists Jan. 20, 2027, as the original deadline for filing a Chapter 11 reorganization plan and disclosure statement. An Oct. 21 hearing memorandum called for a Dec. 21, 2026, deadline, although the memorandum itself states that it is not an official court order.\nWhy it matters: The emergence of another Francis-linked freight business raises questions about what became of R&R’s personnel and operations following its collapse.",
